5417] [17592186108825 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186108825 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "9794165b-ef5e-5984-875b-b0c1f0d7dd57" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186108826 #edb/ref 1080 "q = 2" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186108826 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186108826 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "f1769611-133d-52bf-915b-ce9141431689" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186108827 #edb/ref 1080 "q = ± \\frac{1}{2}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186108827 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186108827 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "53ff716e-3a5f-53b8-aada-da63016943f6" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186108828 #edb/ref 1080 "q = \\frac{1}{2}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186108828 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186108828 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "634a5633-69d7-54ee-a77a-e0058e634f51" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186110346 #edb/ref 1069 "e-14817" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186110346 #edb/ref 1071 "Solve $-2(3k-12)^2 = 0$." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186110346 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "34bac64f-5afc-5c30-bb87-15b3df40f8a9" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186110346 #edb/ref 1074 "We can solve this equation using the square root method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n-2(3k - 12)^{2} &= 0 \\\\\n(3k - 12)^{2} &= 0 \\\\\n\\sqrt{(3k - 12)^{2}} &= \\sqrt{0} \\\\\n\\mid 3k - 12 \\mid &= 0 \\\\\n3k - 12 &= 0 \\\\\n3k &= 12 \\\\\nk &= 4\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186110348 #edb/ref 1069 "e-14820" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186110348 #edb/ref 1071 "Which value of $k$ gives precisely one solution to the equation $(x+3)^2+10=k$?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186110348 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "b9db498b-f1e3-5d52-b23d-106895586815" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186110348 #edb/ref 1074 "First, we solve for $x$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(x + 3)^{2} + 10 &= k \\\\\n(x + 3)^{2} &= k - 10 \\\\\n\\sqrt{(x + 3)^{2}} &= \\sqrt{k - 10} \\\\\n\\mid x + 3 \\mid &= \\sqrt{k - 10}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe equation has only one solution if the right-hand side is equal to zero. So, we solve for the value of $k$ that makes the right-hand side equal to zero:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\sqrt{k - 10} &= 0 \\\\\nk - 10 &= 0 \\\\\nk &= 10\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $k=10$ will give only one solution to the equation." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186110349 #edb/ref 1069 "e-14822" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186110349 #edb/ref 1071 "Given that $g^2 -18g + 81 = 0$, what are the values of $g$?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186110349 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "281c39c4-7b52-51aa-aad8-dbd40223b796" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186110349 #edb/ref 1074 "First, notice that the left-hand side is a perfect square. It just needs to be factored:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ng^{2} - 18g + 81 &= 0 \\\\\ng^{2} - 2 \\cdot 9 \\cdot g + 9^{2} &= 0 \\\\\n(g - 9)^{2} &= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom here, we solve the equation using the square root method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\sqrt{(g - 9)^{2}} &= \\sqrt{0} \\\\\n\\mid g - 9 \\mid &= 0 \\\\\ng - 9 &= 0 \\\\\ng &= 9\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186110350 #edb/ref 1069 "e-14824" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186110350 #edb/ref 1071 "Consider the equation $(4x+1)^2 +2=3k$. For which values of $k$ does this equation have no real solutions?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186110350 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "1e0655ad-122c-5d94-8f8b-9e476eaf4d78" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186110350 #edb/ref 1074 "Let's try and solve using the square root method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(4x + 1)^{2} + 2 &= 3k \\\\\n(4x + 1)^{2} &= 3k - 2 \\\\\n\\sqrt{(4x + 1)^{2}} &= \\sqrt{3k - 2} \\\\\n\\mid 4x + 1 \\mid &= \\sqrt{3k - 2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe equation will have no real solutions if the expression under the square root is negative, which gives the following condition:\n$3k-2 < 0$\n\nWe solve this inequality for $k$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}3k - 2 & < 0 \\\\ 3k & < 2 \\\\ k & < \\frac{2}{3}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nTherefore, any value of $k$ that is less than\n\n$$\n\\dfrac 2 3\n$$\n\nwill give an equation with no real solutions." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186110612 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186048963 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186110612 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186110972 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186110612 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186111183 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186110612 #edb/ref 1039 "Cases When the Perfect Square is Isolated" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186110612 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186110346 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186110612 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "637d4fe0-abb6-5050-a541-f132e78f9b8a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186110613 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186110958 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186110613 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186111002 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186110613 #edb/ref 1039 "Finding an Unknown Parameter" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186110613 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186110348 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186110613 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "2aca8019-3239-539c-b7a9-e1a9ca461a61" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186110614 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186049026 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186110614 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186110973 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186110614 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186111395 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186110614 #edb/ref 1039 "Factoring a Perfect Square" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186110614 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186110349 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186110614 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "f637b472-4c1a-511b-9820-3e07f659eb25" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186110615 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186050503 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186110615 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186110959 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186110615 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186110974 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186110615 #edb/ref 1039 "Equations With No Real Solutions" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186110615 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186110350 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186110615 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "02785337-efe0-54cb-9433-46ff4a9c6302" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186110817 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186111517 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186110817 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186111518 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186110817 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186111519 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186110817 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186111520 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186110817 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186111521 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186110817 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186111522 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186110817 #edb/ref 1044 #edb/ref 17592186045463 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186110817 #edb/ref 1045 "Perfect Square Quadratic Equations with One or No Solutions" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186110817 #edb/ref 1046 #edb/ref 17592186111517 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186110817 #edb/ref 1049 #edb/ref 17592186049668 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186110817 #edb/ref 1053 #uuid "f6c3553a-0bb2-5e80-aaec-94cddf828cfd" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111183 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186108823 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111183 #edb/ref 1069 "q-44355" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111183 #edb/ref 1071 "Solve $(2q + 1)^{2} = 0$." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111183 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "4515a99f-af9e-5afd-8162-572a1dd1a9d7" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111517 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186111518 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111517 #edb/ref 1061 169632 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111517 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "5f5183e9-19ad-5c69-807b-510db46d8281" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111517 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186111919 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111518 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186111519 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111518 #edb/ref 1061 169633 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111518 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "6967ba33-4930-52a7-a7b4-7ffe0762be89" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111518 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186110612 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111519 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186111520 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111519 #edb/ref 1061 169636 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111519 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "bedc7efe-42c1-54d6-8c86-490434760721" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111519 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186110613 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111520 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186111521 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111520 #edb/ref 1061 169639 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111520 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "8ba31f89-a15d-5135-be97-97d5978bcacc" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111520 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186110614 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111521 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186111522 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111521 #edb/ref 1061 169642 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111521 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "e56ff91b-5edd-5f4c-9d04-0fdf7c34e322" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111521 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186111920 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111522 #edb/ref 1061 169643 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111522 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "c852bd3c-c398-5a0c-9830-c6b1283797c0" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111522 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186110615 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111919 #edb/ref 1064 "Many quadratic equations have two solutions. However, it's also possible for a quadratic equation to have only one solution or no real solutions at all.\n\nFor example, consider the following quadratic equation:\n\n$$\n(3x-6)^2=0\n$$\n\nWe can solve this equation using the square root method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(3x - 6)^{2} &= 0 \\\\\n\\sqrt{(3x - 6)^{2}} &= \\sqrt{0} \\\\\n\\mid 3x - 6 \\mid &= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNormally, at this stage, we would obtain two solutions by setting the inside of the absolute value equal to positive or negative $(\\pm)$ the right-hand side. However, in this case, the right-hand side is zero, and $+0$ and $-0$ are both just $0$.\n\nSo, we have only one solution:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\mid 3x - 6 \\mid &= 0 \\\\\n3x - 6 &= 0 \\\\\n3x &= 6 \\\\\nx &= 2\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111919 #edb/ref 1065 9203 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111919 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "3b3899a9-dbc6-524a-b493-c57a45bfeda9" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111919 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111920 #edb/ref 1064 "Let's try and solve the equation\n\n$$\n(x+1)^2=-1\n$$\n\nusing the square root method.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(x + 1)^{2} &= -1 \\\\\n\\sqrt{(x + 1)^{2}} &= \\sqrt{-1} \\\\\n\\mid x + 1 \\mid &= \\sqrt{-1}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe have a problem here. We cannot take the square root of a negative number. For that reason, the equation above has *no real solutions*." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111920 #edb/ref 1065 9205 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111920 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "414b731c-3f76-57ad-985a-3d64bda214e0" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111920 #edb/ref 1067 "Perfect Square Quadratic Equations With No Real Solutions" #edb/ref 17592186045417]}
